  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nA weak cold front will move through South Texas this afternoon, \nushering in a drier air mass and slightly cooler overnight \ntemperatures. Mostly dry conditions will persist through Friday as \nsurface high pressure briefly influences the region. Onshore flow \nreturns Friday night as the high shifts east, allowing moisture to \nincrease ahead of an approaching upper-level disturbance.\n\nRain chances increase over the weekend, with the greatest coverage \nexpected Sunday afternoon into the evening. Precipitation chances \npeak near 85% across the Victoria Crossroads, where widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms are anticipated. GEFS mean PWATs around \n1.30-1.40 inches indicate sufficient moisture to support rainfall, \nalthough the potential for locally heavy rainfall will depend on \navailable instability and the strength of the forcing.\n\nUncertainty remains regarding timing of the next cold front, with \nthe Euro bringing the the boundary to the region around 00Z Monday \nwhile the GFS favors a later arrival around 12Z Monday. This timing \ndifference could influence the duration and coverage of \nprecipitation Sunday night into Monday. Drier conditions are \nexpected following FROPA, with a gradual return of onshore flow \npossible by the middle of next week. \n\nIncreasing onshore flow, elevated wave heights, and longer-period \nswells of 8-9 seconds will contribute to increased coastal hazards \nlate Saturday into Sunday. P-ETSS guidance indicates tidal levels \nreaching 1.9-2.2 feet MSL, suggesting the potential for minor \ncoastal flooding along portions of the Middle Texas coast. An \nincreased risk of rip currents is also expected during this period. \nCoastal conditions will continue to be monitored for possible \nadvisory issuance.",
